ark and cordon to minimise disturbance  of course a bird as rare and beautiful  as a bee eater attempting to breed in  the uk is going to draw a bit of a crowd  but its presence here is significant for  another reason and that's because of  what it says about our rapidly warming  climate  beta's made an attempt to breed in the  uk in 1950 but since 2000 there have  been six nesting attempts  the last in 2017 failed due to bad  weather it really is hit or miss because  you know they are arriving in a country  that they previously haven't bred in um  and so you know it's  quite stressful for them as a species  it's a clear link between climate and  nature crisis they've got to you know  obviously feed themselves um and we are  you know in a very nature depleted  country um  so you know if there is then a  kind of large population in the uk we  have to take that on as world leaders  and and protect those those species  while the climate may be shifting in  their favor experience tells the rspb  over-eager twitchers or worse egg  collectors might disturb the birds  so they're now watching the nest site 24  hours a day letting nature take its  course through our changing times  tom clark sky news north norfolk  well as drought worsens across the horn  of africa threatening the lives and  livelihoods of millions of people a  senior kenyan official says it's  disappointing that developed nations  aren't honoring their climate finance  pledges the comments from the kenyan  official responsible for tackling  climate change comes as a new report  reveals rich nations are breaking  promises over climate finance in 2009  wealthy nations promised that by 2020  they would provide a hundred billion  dollars every year  in funding to help developing nations  mitigate against and adapt to climate  change well that target is not being met  and the report from the care climate  justice center says that over the past  decade nearly half of the funds reported  as climate finance by g7 n